# IRAM placement for the receive path of the digital link.
#
# esps_dio_rx_push_bit() and esps_dio_crc8() are called from the RX_CLK
# interrupt handler, which is registered with ESP_INTR_FLAG_IRAM so that it
# keeps firing while the flash cache is disabled. Code in flash is unreachable
# in that window, so calling a flash-resident function from such a handler is
# not "slow", it is a crash.
#
# Why the handler needs that flag at all: this node writes NVS (the boot-count
# commit at startup, node.set_label from the station), and every flash write
# disables the cache for hundreds of microseconds to milliseconds. A bit period
# is 100 us at the default 10 kbit/s, so a masked handler would swallow whole
# runs of clock edges. The receiver's shift register would then hold half of
# one frame and half of the next: frames_err up, BER up, and no indication
# anywhere that the cause was this node writing its own flash rather than the
# cable being measured. A link-quality instrument that injects its own errors
# invisibly is worse than none.
#
# Object granularity, not symbol granularity: esps_dio_rx_init() is also
# reached from the handler (the resync request), and these two objects are
# small enough that pulling esps_dio_frame_build() and the txbits iterator in
# with them costs a few hundred bytes of IRAM and removes any chance of
# missing a callee.
#
# The placement lives here rather than as IRAM_ATTR in the sources because
# IRAM_ATTR comes from an ESP-IDF header, and D-4 keeps the pure C11 half of
# esps_dio compiling under plain gcc for the host tests. Placement is a
# build-system concern; the code stays portable.

[mapping:esps_dio_iram]
archive: libesps_dio.a
entries:
    frame (noflash)
    crc8 (noflash)
